date: 2026-09-17T00:00:00-04:00
---

The Harlequins were the builders of the Strider and the organizers of the pilgrimage.

The [[Org/The Harlequins/Providence|Providence]], the strider at [[Geography/Calaria/Ragged Roost|Ragged Roost]], is their work: a platform on eight legs with buildings on top.

At a pilgrimage the Harlequins wore red-and-green pinstriped shirts, bell-capped masks whose shadow hides the line of the neck, and gloves. They took invitations at the head of the line and handed out plain white masks to guests. Others walked the street with platters of champagne and hors d'oeuvres, ran the attractions, or mopped the arena floor after a fight.

They ran games along the main street and the paths out of town: a fortune teller's booth, an association game on the fairy-ring path, a barker before a rainbow prism with a key at its center, and a table of dice. Winning a game pays a hint about a [[Phenomena/Keystone|keystone]].

Once the guests were aboard, a Harlequin such as [[Org/The Harlequins/People/Chuck|Chuck]] was assigned as servant to each berth, dressed more richly than the rest and carrying a scepter, and given whatever name its guests chose. The Harlequins also kept orts, the least of the devils, as laborers, walking them on leashes through the town and up onto the strider.

The town the Harlequins kept at the front of the strider held its own [[Org/The Harlequins/Harlequin Town|library and clock tower]], and [[Org/The Harlequins/People/Ocelith|Ocelith]] was its curator.

Below the guest decks, a population of contract workers lives in the strider's cramped inner halls, bound to it by debts with interest that the Harlequins held.

The order has been shattered. The town at the prow was overrun by void creatures and torn apart by the same machinery the Harlequins used to carry travelers toward the void, and many of its people are dead there. Their servant [[Org/The Harlequins/People/Chuck|Chuck]] died where he stood.

The whole pilgrimage, it is now known, was run for the god [[Divinity/Ashen Pact/War|War]], a thing living outside the firmament. The Harlequins did War's bidding aboard the strider, and its liaison in turn approached the family of [[Org/Amber Call/index|Amber Call]] with work the costumed pilgrims could not do.

In the tower that carries the strider’s helm, some of them have been corrupted: bent out of humanoid shape, their masks grown into the bone of the face.
